# 8. Demand → Staffing Requirement

For the MVP, avoid introducing an unnecessary machine-learning system immediately.

Start with deterministic demand bands.

Example:

```text
Kitchen staffing

Orders / hour      Required cooks

0–15                    2
16–30                   3
31–50                   4
51–70                   5
71+                     6
```

Similar mappings exist for:

- service staff,
- cashiers,
- baristas,
- preparation staff.

The staffing requirement for every time block becomes:

```text
staffing_requirement(
    outlet,
    role,
    time_interval
)
```

Later this can be generated from a forecasting model.

---

# 9. Weekly Schedule Generation

This is the flagship feature.

Manager chooses:

```text
Outlet:
Indiranagar

Week:
19–25 October

Optimization priority:
Balanced
```

Clicks:

> Generate Schedule

ShiftPilot solves the scheduling problem and returns:

```text
Schedule generated

Coverage                    98.7%
Labour cost               ₹43,840
Overtime                     ₹620
Availability conflicts          0
Rule violations                 0
Preference match              91%
```

The user then reviews the roster before publishing it.

---

# 10. Real-Time Schedule Repair

This is the second flagship feature.

Suppose Ravi reports:

> I cannot work today.

Ravi has:

```text
18:00–23:00
Grill
Indiranagar
```

ShiftPilot calculates the consequences.

```text
SHIFT AT RISK

Role:
Grill

Expected dinner demand:
High

Without replacement:

Coverage           72%
Estimated wait     14.1 min
```

The repair engine explores feasible alternatives.

### Plan A

```text
Assign Arjun

Additional cost       ₹180
Schedule changes         1
Overtime                 No
Skill match             100%
Travel                  22m
```

### Plan B

```text
Extend Neha

Additional cost       ₹360
Schedule changes         1
Overtime                 No
Rest risk               Medium
```

### Plan C

```text
Rebalance current team

Neha → Grill
Aman → Prep
Karan +60m

Additional cost        ₹90
Schedule changes         3
Overtime                 No
```

ShiftPilot recommends one.

The manager approves.

---

# 11. Schedule Stability

This should be a first-class optimization objective.

A mathematically optimal solution is not necessarily operationally useful.

If one employee calls in sick, ShiftPilot should not redesign twenty unrelated shifts simply because doing so saves ₹500.

Define:

```text
schedule_disruption =
number of modified existing assignments
+
weighted change in start/end times
+
outlet transfers
+
employee reassignment penalties
```

Repair optimization therefore becomes:

```text
minimize

labour_cost
+
overtime
+
understaffing
+
preference_violations
+
schedule_disruption
```

The repair engine strongly favors keeping existing commitments unchanged.

---

# 12. Hard Constraints

Hard constraints can never be violated.

Examples:

## Availability

An employee cannot be assigned when unavailable.

```text
assignment[e,t] <= availability[e,t]
```

---

## Skill requirements

A grill shift can only be filled by an employee with grill capability.

```text
employee.skills contains shift.required_skill
```

---

## Overlapping shifts

Employees cannot work two overlapping shifts.

---

## Maximum working hours

Example:

```text
weekly_hours(employee) <= 48
```

unless the organization explicitly enables overtime.

---

## Minimum rest period

Example:

```text
next_shift.start - previous_shift.end >= 10 hours
```

---

## Outlet feasibility

If an employee works:

```text
Koramangala
10:00–17:00
```

and the travel time to Indiranagar is 35 minutes:

```text
earliest feasible Indiranagar start = 17:35
```

---

## Required role eligibility

Cashier assignment requires cashier eligibility.

A cook cannot automatically fill it simply because they are available.

---

# 13. Soft Constraints

Soft constraints may be violated when necessary but receive penalties.

Examples:

- employee shift preferences,
- preferred number of weekly hours,
- preferred outlet,
- fairness,
- overtime,
- unusual shift times,
- cross-outlet movement,
- last-minute changes.

The optimizer can trade these against one another.

---

# 14. Objective Function

Conceptually:

```text
Minimize:

W1 × Labour Cost
+
W2 × Overtime Cost
+
W3 × Understaffing Penalty
+
W4 × Preference Violation
+
W5 × Schedule Disruption
+
W6 × Cross-Outlet Transfer Cost
+
W7 × Fairness Penalty
```

The exact weights remain internal.

The normal user should never need to configure mathematical weights.

Instead provide human-readable strategies:

```text
Optimization Mode

○ Lowest Cost
● Balanced
○ Staff Friendly
○ Maximum Coverage
```

These map internally to objective weights.

---

# 15. Solver

Recommended implementation:

## Google OR-Tools CP-SAT

Why CP-SAT:

- scheduling is naturally constraint-heavy,
- supports Boolean assignment variables,
- handles hard and soft constraints,
- supports optimization,
- provides strong performance for MVP scale,
- supports infeasibility reasoning patterns,
- is an established engineering choice rather than an LLM hack.

The LLM must never decide the final schedule.

The architecture is:

```text
Natural language
        ↓
Structured intent / constraints
        ↓
Validated scheduling model
        ↓
CP-SAT
        ↓
Feasible solutions
        ↓
Ranking / explanation
        ↓
Manager approval
```

---

# 16. Explainable Optimization

ShiftPilot should never return:

> The optimizer selected Arjun.

Instead:

```text
WHY ARJUN?

✓ Available 6–11 PM
✓ Grill trained
✓ No conflicting shift
✓ 38 weekly hours after assignment
✓ No overtime
✓ 22 minutes from outlet
✓ Worked this role previously

Alternative candidates

Neha
Rejected: minimum rest violation

Aman
Rejected: overtime threshold

Priya
Rejected: missing grill skill
```

The explanation must come from deterministic solver facts.

The LLM may convert those facts into friendly language, but it must not invent the reasoning.

---

# 17. Infeasible Schedule Handling

One of the strongest parts of ShiftPilot should be how it handles impossible schedules.

Do not display:

```text
Solver status: INFEASIBLE
```

Instead:

```text
I couldn't create a schedule that satisfies every rule.

Friday 7–10 PM requires:

5 grill cooks

Available qualified employees:

3
```

Then ShiftPilot finds the smallest constraint relaxations.

Example:

```text
Option A

Allow Aman 2 hours overtime
Cost: +₹420


Option B

Bring Ravi from Koramangala
Travel: 32 minutes
Cost: +₹180


Option C

Run with 4 cooks

Expected coverage: 88%
Estimated wait increase: +2.8 min
```

Manager chooses which tradeoff is acceptable.

---

# 18. Natural-Language Rule Engine

This is one place where an LLM adds meaningful usability.

A manager should be able to write:

> Students shouldn't work after 9 PM on weekdays.

The model converts this to:

```json
{
  "scope": {
    "employee_tag": "student",
    "days": ["MON", "TUE", "WED", "THU", "FRI"]
  },
  "constraint": {
    "shift_end_before": "21:00"
  }
}
```

Before saving, ShiftPilot shows:

```text
New scheduling rule

Applies to:
Employees tagged "Student"

Days:
Monday–Friday

Rule:
Shift must end by 9 PM

Affected employees:
6

[Save rule]
```

The manager confirms it.

Only then does it become a deterministic solver constraint.

---

# 19. Natural-Language Commands

AI chat should not dominate the product.

Instead provide a universal command box:

```text
Ask ShiftPilot...
```

Examples:

> Give Ravi Saturday off.

> Find someone to cover Neha tonight.

> Try reducing overtime next week.

> Keep more experienced people during Friday dinner.

> Don't move anyone between outlets this weekend.

> Make the schedule cheaper without reducing dinner coverage.

Each command gets translated into a structured scheduling operation.

Example:

```text
User:
Give Ravi Saturday off.
```

ShiftPilot:

```text
This affects one shift.

Ravi
Saturday
16:00–23:00

Recommended replacement:

Arjun
16:00–23:00

Cost difference:
+₹120

Coverage:
unchanged

[Apply]
```

---

# 20. User Experience Philosophy

ShiftPilot should feel like scheduling software that happens to be intelligent.

Not:

> an AI chatbot that happens to know about schedules.

The key UX rule is:

> **No critical workflow should require writing a prompt.**

Prompts are shortcuts.

Core functionality must work through normal UI interactions.

---

# 21. Schedule-First Interface

The homepage is the schedule.

Not an analytics dashboard.

Not AI chat.

Not graphs.

Example:

```text
