# 23. Drag-and-Drop Editing

Managers must retain manual control.

Allow:

- moving shifts,
- resizing shifts,
- duplicating shifts,
- assigning employees,
- changing roles.

ShiftPilot validates edits instantly.

Example valid edit:

```text
✓ Ravi is available
✓ No overlap
✓ Rest period satisfied
✓ No overtime

Coverage
86% → 93%
```

Example invalid edit:

```text
This shift creates a conflict.

Ravi works:

Koramangala
10:00–17:00

Travel time:
38 minutes

Earliest feasible start:
17:45
```

Do not silently reject changes.

Explain them.

---

# 24. Locked Shifts

Managers should be able to lock:

- one shift,
- one employee's day,
- an entire day,
- a published section of the schedule.

Example:

```text
Ravi

Saturday
14:00–22:00    🔒
```

The optimizer treats locked shifts as hard constraints.

This gives users confidence that optimization will not unexpectedly rearrange decisions they care about.

---

# 25. Before / After Optimization

Optimization should never silently replace the roster.

Show comparison:

```text
I found a better schedule.

                      CURRENT       PROPOSED

Coverage                91%            98%
Labour Cost           ₹46,400        ₹43,900
Overtime               ₹3,200          ₹800
Conflicts                   3              0
Preference Match          79%            90%

Estimated saving:
₹2,500
```

Then show the actual changes.

```text
7 changes

Ravi
Tue 10–6 → Tue 12–8

Reason:
better dinner coverage


Neha
Fri OFF → Fri 4–10

Reason:
fills kitchen shortage


Aman
Fri 4–11 → Fri 3–10

Reason:
avoids overtime
```

Actions:

```text
[Apply all]

[Review individually]

[Discard]
```

